<urn:atm:triage:finding/AV-HOTFIX-003>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "AV-HOTFIX-003" ;
  triage:title "Client/daemon compatibility preflight is dead code and the peer path has no version check" ;
  triage:description "atm-daemon-client/src/compatibility.rs verify_connection_compatibility is pub-exported from lib.rs:33 but has zero production callers in crates/atm, atm-daemon-client, or atm-graft-python (confirmed via rg across crates, non-test); the only trace in the CLI is the exit-code mapping for ClientDaemonVersionIncompatible in crates/atm/src/main.rs:138. So no CLI-to-daemon mismatch is ever flagged. Also the constants have been CLI_SCHEMA_VERSION=1 and HTTP_API_VERSION=1.0.0 since before 1.4.13 (same at prerelease/v1.4.13, v1.5.0, and integrate/phase-aw), so even a live preflight would call 1.4.13 and 1.5.1 compatible -- the version-skew detection has never actually been exercised. The peer client (client.rs, peer_connection_pool.rs) sends no preflight and no version field at all; a wire-format drift between hosts would surface as a parse error on the receiving daemon rather than a clear compatibility diagnostic." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-av" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "arch-gate" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:repeatable false ;
  triage:sweepScope "file_only" ;
  triage:status "fixed" ;
  triage:closedAt "2026-09-05T21:40:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:closedBy "quality-mgr" ;
  triage:dispatchReady false ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-09-05T18:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:foundIn triage:AV4 ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-09-05T18:20:58Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AV-HOTFIX-003/AV4-1> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AV-HOTFIX-003/AV4-2> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AV-HOTFIX-003/AV4-3> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AV-HOTFIX-003/AV4-4> ;
  triage:triageRecordVersion "1" .

# RULING (Rand, relayed via fenix, 2026-09-05T19:10Z), APPROVED fix
# direction -- still triage:dispatchReady false:
# schema validation for cross-host messages: the peer write envelope must
# carry a schema/API version; the receiver rejects a major-version mismatch
# with a dedicated error naming both versions, and tolerates minor skew.
# Semver assumption: extensions are minor and must be tolerated; breaking
# changes are major. Rand also identified a PROCESS gap: there is no defined
# process for when a breaking change to the cross-host interface is
# allowed. That is a planned item, not a bug in this finding -- tracked as
# GitHub issue #1217 ("Planned process: semver-versioned cross-host
# interface and breaking-change approval (needs a plan)"); see that issue
# for the process design, not this record.
# The other proposed items in this finding (wire up the dead CLI preflight,
# doctor version reporting, an envelope-change guard test) are NOT yet
# explicitly ruled -- list as proposed only, not approved.
#
# CLARIFICATION (Rand, relayed via fenix, 2026-09-05T19:10Z; also added as a
# comment on issue #1217): an optional parameter added to a command is a
# MINOR bump of the HTTP schema version (must be tolerated by older peers).
# Only removal or a semantic change of an existing field is a MAJOR bump.
#
# STANDING RULE ADDENDUM (Rand, relayed via fenix, 2026-09-05T19:11Z),
# effective immediately, applies to every PR gate (not just this finding):
# the real risk is agents making a breaking wire change without Rand's
# approval. Any change to the wire shape of the HTTP or peer schema requires
# the matching version bump; a MAJOR (breaking) bump additionally requires
# Rand's explicit recorded approval before merge -- quality-mgr must treat an
# unapproved breaking wire change as blocking. The eventual plan (#1217) must
# turn this into a mechanical CI gate; until then it is an explicit manual
# quality-mgr review rule.
#
# RULING (Rand, relayed via fenix, 2026-09-05T19:20Z), APPROVED fix scope --
# folded into AV-HOTFIX-003's dispatch scope:
# No 2.0 release. v1.0 is already in the wild and current code is compatible
# with it. Set HTTP_API_VERSION to 1.1.0, defined as exactly today's schema
# (all additive changes since 1.0.0 folded in as the 1.1.0 baseline); bump on
# every future change from here per the semver rules above. Rand's own audit:
# zero wire changes between v1.4.13 and 1.5.1; the PeerSync removal
# (2026-08-03) is the only non-additive change since 1.0.0, and no fleet host
# predates it, so 1.1.0 is safe to declare as the current baseline without a
# major bump.

